// x86-64 (System V) codegen for the baseline JIT, beside src/neant/jit/arm64.nt (src/jit.rs owns
// memory/execution; see docs/compiler.md "Stage 2" and its x86-64 subsection). Two entry points, named apart
// from the AArch64 ones so both backends can be in the boot image at once and src/jit.rs picks the
// one its target arch needs:
//
// jitCompileX86[input;trampolines] -> (Bytes; Ints) or null, exactly jitCompile's contract.
// jitCompileTraceX86[input] -> (Bytes; Ints; Ints; Int; List) or null, exactly jitCompileTrace's.
//
// Nothing here runs at load time, so where this file sits in BOOT_FILES (src/main.rs) doesn't
// matter: the arm64.nt helpers below are resolved when a compile actually happens.
//
// Everything that decides *whether* a function or a trace compiles is arm64.nt's and is called
// from here rather than copied: jitClassifySlots, jitLocalSlots, jitDyadKind, jitTraceTouched,
// jitTrPos, jitTrTag/jitTrTagCodes/jitTrSameTags, jitMAXDEPTH, jitTrMAXTOUCHED. The compilability
// walk, the slot classification and the deopt/guard points are therefore the same code, not the
// same code twice: the two backends accept and reject exactly the same inputs, and a change to the
// subset can't drift between them. What is new here is the instruction encoders, the register
// assignment, and how branches are resolved.
//
// ---- registers (System V AMD64): the roles arm64.nt documents, mapped onto this ABI.
// Long-lived pointers must survive the `call`s a compiled function makes, so they go in
// callee-saved registers (rbx, rbp, r12-r15 — the trampolines preserve them for free, exactly as
// x19..x22 are preserved on AArch64); the operand stack is caller-saved and spilled around every
// call, the way x9..x14 are.
//
//   role                     AArch64      x86-64            why
//   loc (`buf`) pointer      x19          rbx               callee-saved: held across calls
//   int-null sentinel        x20          rbp               callee-saved: ditto. Not a frame
//                                                           pointer here — nothing unwinds
//                                                           through this code, and the ABI only
//                                                           asks that it be restored, which the
//                                                           epilogue does.
//   vm pointer               x21          r12               callee-saved
//   ok-out pointer           x22          r13               callee-saved
//   operand stack (6 deep)   x9..x14      rsi,rdi,r8..r11   caller-saved, spilled around a call.
//                                                           Register numbers 6,7,8,9,10,11 are
//                                                           consecutive, so `jitxSTACK+i` indexes
//                                                           it the same way `jitSTACK+i` does.
//   call scratch             x15          rax               holds the trampoline address for
//                                                           `call rax`, then its return value
//   post-call ok scratch     x15          rcx               reads the ok word back after a call
//   locals in registers      x23..x28,    r14, r15          only two are left after the four
//                            x6..x8,                        long-lived roles above, and both are
//                            x16,x17                        callee-saved, so — unlike AArch64 —
//                                                           there are no caller-saved local
//                                                           registers and nothing to spill around
//                                                           a call. A third local and beyond stays
//                                                           in `buf` and is reached through rbx,
//                                                           exactly as arm64.nt's overflow slots
//                                                           are (jitxLocalToReg); no function is
//                                                           rejected for being too wide, its extra
//                                                           locals just run the old way.
//   trace buffer / out ptr   x0, x1       rdi, rsi          the trace tier's two arguments, held
//                                                           for its whole lifetime (see below)
//
// The six argument registers this ABI uses for a call (rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8, r9) overlap the
// operand stack, which AArch64's x0..x5 / x9..x14 split does not. So a call here spills *every*
// live operand-stack register, not just the ones below the operands the call consumes, and then
// loads the arguments it needs out of those spill slots — there is no order in which the register
// moves alone are safe. It costs a handful of memory operations on a path that is already making
// a call, and it removes the whole class of "an argument clobbered an operand that hadn't been
// read yet" bug.
//
// ---- branches: fixed-width rel32, emitted once, patched once.
// An x86 instruction is variable length, so how far a jump reaches depends on the size of what it
// jumps over — and a displacement that fits in a byte would make the instruction shorter, which
// could in turn change another jump's distance. Nothing here plays that game: **every** branch is
// emitted in its rel32 form (`jmp` = E9 + rel32, `jcc` = 0F 8x + rel32), never the rel8 short
// form, so an instruction's length is a function of its *form* alone and never of its operands'
// values. That makes the standard two-pass resolution exact: pass one emits the instruction in
// full with a zero displacement and records the byte offset of that 4-byte field (jitxPatch32's
// callers); pass two subtracts and writes four bytes, moving nothing. The same choice is why every
// memory operand uses a full disp32 and every constant the 10-byte `mov r64, imm64`.
//
// `asm` is therefore a list of plain byte values (ints 0..255) rather than arm64.nt's list of
// 32-bit words, and `count asm` is a byte offset — which is what every fixup, every `ipByte` entry
// and every stub position below is. The final `` `byte$asm `` is the Bytes value src/jit.rs maps.
//
// A hex literal in this language is a Bytes vector, not an int (see arm64.nt's jitW), so an opcode
// byte is written `` `int$0x8b `` and a two-byte opcode `` `int$0x0faf `` — which is already the
// two-element list these encoders want to append.

jitxLOC: 3          // rbx
jitxNI: 5           // rbp
jitxVM: 12          // r12
jitxOK: 13          // r13
jitxSTACK: 6        // rsi, rdi, r8..r11 (consecutive register numbers 6..11)
jitxSCRATCH: 0      // rax
jitxOKTMP: 1        // rcx
jitxSP: 4           // rsp
jitxLREGS: 14 15    // both callee-saved, so no local is ever spilled around a call

// Pushed in the prologue and popped in the epilogue, always all six even when a role's register
// goes unused: a fixed, even count is what makes the 16-byte stack alignment below an argument
// this file can state once instead of per compiled function.
jitxSAVED: 3 5 12 13 14 15

// Frame: 48 bytes of operand-stack spill (jitMAXDEPTH words) then the ok word a trampoline writes.
// Sized to 72 rather than 64 for alignment: this code is entered with rsp ≡ 8 (mod 16) — the
// `call` into it pushed a return address — the six pushes take 48 more, and System V wants
// rsp ≡ 0 (mod 16) *at* a call instruction, so the frame has to be ≡ 8 (mod 16) itself.
jitxSPILLBASE: 0
jitxCALLOK: 48
jitxFRAME: 72

// Condition codes, as the low nibble of a jcc (0x80+cc), setcc (0x90+cc) or cmovcc (0x40+cc).
jitxCcE: 4
jitxCcNE: 5
jitxCcA: 7          // unsigned above: CF=0 and ZF=0 — the ordered float compare, see jitxTrDyadFCmp
jitxCcNP: 11        // not-parity: the float compare was ordered (neither operand a NaN)
jitxCcL: 12
jitxCcLE: 14
jitxCcG: 15

// ---- instruction encoders. Every one was cross-checked byte for byte against `nasm -f bin` output
// for the same mnemonic and operands (tests/x86.nt asserts those bytes, and docs/compiler.md "Stage 2"'s
// x86-64 subsection says what that does and does not prove), and read back with
// `llvm-objdump -D -b binary -m x86-64`.

// REX prefix: W (64-bit operand), R (the ModRM reg field names r8..r15), B (its rm field does).
jitxRex: {[w;reg;rm] (((jitW 0x40) bor ($[w;8;0])) bor ($[reg>7;4;0])) bor ($[rm>7;1;0])}
// ModRM, register-direct (mod=11): reg and rm both name registers.
jitxModRR: {[reg;rm] ((jitW 0xc0) bor ((reg band 7) shl 3)) bor (rm band 7)}
// ModRM for [base+disp32] (mod=10) plus, when the rm field would otherwise mean "there is a SIB
// byte here" (rm=100, i.e. rsp or r12), that SIB byte — 0x24 is scale=1, no index, base=rm.
jitxMem: {[reg;base;disp]
  m: ((jitW 0x80) bor ((reg band 7) shl 3)) bor (base band 7)
  $[4=(base band 7); (m; jitW 0x24),jitxLE32 disp; (enlist m),jitxLE32 disp]}

jitxLE32: {[v] (v band 255; (v shr 8) band 255; (v shr 16) band 255; (v shr 24) band 255)}
jitxLE64: {[v] (jitxLE32 v),jitxLE32 (v shr 32)}

// mov rd, imm64 — always the 10-byte form, whatever the value (see the header comment on widths).
jitxMovRI: {[rd;v] ((jitxRex[1;0;rd]; (jitW 0xb8)+(rd band 7)),jitxLE64 v)}
// mov rd, rs  (89 /r is `mov r/m64, r64`, so the *destination* is the rm field)
jitxMovRR: {[rd;rs] (jitxRex[1;rs;rd]; jitW 0x89; jitxModRR[rs;rd])}
// mov rd, [base+disp]  /  mov [base+disp], rs
jitxLoad: {[rd;base;disp] (jitxRex[1;rd;base]; jitW 0x8b),jitxMem[rd;base;disp]}
jitxStore: {[base;disp;rs] (jitxRex[1;rs;base]; jitW 0x89),jitxMem[rs;base;disp]}
// The reg/reg ALU forms that take `op r/m64, r64`: destination in the rm field, like mov above.
jitxAlu: {[op;rd;rs] (jitxRex[1;rs;rd]; op; jitxModRR[rs;rd])}
jitxAddRR: {[rd;rs] jitxAlu[jitW 0x01;rd;rs]}
jitxSubRR: {[rd;rs] jitxAlu[jitW 0x29;rd;rs]}
jitxAndRR: {[rd;rs] jitxAlu[jitW 0x21;rd;rs]}
// `or` and (below) `divsd`, `movq r64 <- xmm` are the siblings of encoders this backend does use,
// kept and pinned byte for byte by tests/x86.nt but not reachable from the compilable subset: the
// integer `&`/`|` verbs are min/max rather than bitwise (so `and` earns its place only in the float
// equality sequence), and neither tier compiles a division.
jitxOrRR: {[rd;rs] jitxAlu[jitW 0x09;rd;rs]}
jitxCmpRR: {[rd;rs] jitxAlu[jitW 0x39;rd;rs]}
jitxTestRR: {[rd;rs] jitxAlu[jitW 0x85;rd;rs]}
// imul, unlike the above, is `imul r64, r/m64`: the destination is the *reg* field.
jitxImulRR: {[rd;rs] (jitxRex[1;rd;rs]),(`int$0x0faf),jitxModRR[rd;rs]}
// The /digit group-1 immediate forms: cmp rd, imm8 and sub rd, imm8 (sign-extended).
jitxCmpRI8: {[rd;imm] (jitxRex[1;0;rd]; jitW 0x83; ((jitW 0xf8) bor (rd band 7)); imm band 255)}
jitxSubRI8: {[rd;imm] (jitxRex[1;0;rd]; jitW 0x83; ((jitW 0xe8) bor (rd band 7)); imm band 255)}
// sub/add rsp, imm32 — the frame, so imm32 whatever the size (jitxFRAME fits a byte today).
jitxSubSP: {[imm] (jitxRex[1;0;jitxSP]; jitW 0x81; (jitW 0xe8) bor jitxSP),jitxLE32 imm}
jitxAddSP: {[imm] (jitxRex[1;0;jitxSP]; jitW 0x81; (jitW 0xc0) bor jitxSP),jitxLE32 imm}
// lea rd, [rsp+disp] — how a trampoline gets `&ok`, where AArch64 uses `add xd, sp, #imm`.
jitxLeaSP: {[rd;disp] (jitxRex[1;rd;jitxSP]; jitW 0x8d),jitxMem[rd;jitxSP;disp]}
// setcc r8: the low byte of rd. A REX prefix is needed (and only needed) from register 4 up —
// without one, rm=4..7 would mean ah/ch/dh/bh instead of spl/bpl/sil/dil.
jitxSetcc: {[cc;rd]
  t: ((`int$0x0f),((jitW 0x90)+cc)),jitxModRR[0;rd]
  $[rd>3; (enlist jitxRex[0;0;rd]),t; t]}
// movzx rd, rs8 — what turns a setcc's byte into the plain 0/1 int the rest of the code expects.
jitxMovzx: {[rd;rs] (jitxRex[1;rd;rs]),(`int$0x0fb6),jitxModRR[rd;rs]}
// cmovcc rd, rs — destination in the reg field. Reads flags, writes none.
jitxCmovcc: {[cc;rd;rs] (jitxRex[1;rd;rs]),((`int$0x0f),((jitW 0x40)+cc)),jitxModRR[rd;rs]}
// jmp/jcc rel32, with the displacement left zero for jitxPatch32 (see the header comment).
jitxJmp: {[] (jitW 0xe9),jitxLE32 0}
jitxJcc: {[cc] ((`int$0x0f),((jitW 0x80)+cc)),jitxLE32 0}
// call rd — no REX.W: a near call is 64-bit by default in long mode.
jitxCallR: {[rd] $[rd>7; (jitxRex[0;0;rd]; jitW 0xff; (jitW 0xd0) bor (rd band 7)); (jitW 0xff; (jitW 0xd0) bor (rd band 7))]}
jitxPush: {[rd] $[rd>7; (jitxRex[0;0;rd]; (jitW 0x50)+(rd band 7)); enlist (jitW 0x50)+(rd band 7)]}
jitxPop: {[rd] $[rd>7; (jitxRex[0;0;rd]; (jitW 0x58)+(rd band 7)); enlist (jitW 0x58)+(rd band 7)]}
jitxRet: {[] enlist jitW 0xc3}

// ---- SSE2, for the float side of the tracing tier (the whole-function tier is integer-only, so
// none of this is reachable from jitCompileX86). A trace records *observed* types, so as on
// AArch64 there is no static-typing problem to solve here, just the ISA. REX is emitted only when
// an operand is xmm8 or above — these are not 64-bit-operand instructions, so there is no REX.W.
jitxSse2: {[pfx;op;reg;rm]
  r: jitxRex[0;reg;rm]
  body: ((enlist op),jitxModRR[reg;rm])
  $[r=(jitW 0x40); (enlist pfx),(`int$0x0f),body; (pfx; r),(`int$0x0f),body]}
jitxMovsdRR: {[xd;xs] jitxSse2[jitW 0xf2; jitW 0x10; xd; xs]}
jitxAddsd: {[xd;xs] jitxSse2[jitW 0xf2; jitW 0x58; xd; xs]}
jitxSubsd: {[xd;xs] jitxSse2[jitW 0xf2; jitW 0x5c; xd; xs]}
jitxMulsd: {[xd;xs] jitxSse2[jitW 0xf2; jitW 0x59; xd; xs]}
jitxDivsd: {[xd;xs] jitxSse2[jitW 0xf2; jitW 0x5e; xd; xs]}
// ucomisd xa, xb sets ZF/CF/PF from xa against xb, and PF specifically when either is a NaN —
// which is what makes jitxTrDyadFCmp's `seta`/`setnp` reproduce Rust's comparison semantics.
jitxUcomisd: {[xa;xb] jitxSse2[jitW 0x66; jitW 0x2e; xa; xb]}
// movsd xd, [base+disp] / movsd [base+disp], xs — 10 loads, 11 stores.
jitxMovsdMem: {[op;reg;base;disp]
  r: jitxRex[0;reg;base]
  body: ((enlist op),jitxMem[reg;base;disp])
  $[r=(jitW 0x40); ((jitW 0xf2)),(`int$0x0f),body; ((jitW 0xf2); r),(`int$0x0f),body]}
jitxMovsdLoad: {[xd;base;disp] jitxMovsdMem[jitW 0x10;xd;base;disp]}
jitxMovsdStore: {[base;disp;xs] jitxMovsdMem[jitW 0x11;xs;base;disp]}
// movq between an xmm and a GPR, raw bits, both directions (AArch64's FMOV Dd,Xn / FMOV Xd,Dn):
// 66 REX.W 0F 6E moves GPR -> xmm, 66 REX.W 0F 7E xmm -> GPR, the xmm in the reg field either way.
jitxMovqXR: {[xd;rs] ((jitW 0x66); jitxRex[1;xd;rs]),(`int$0x0f6e),jitxModRR[xd;rs]}
jitxMovqRX: {[rd;xs] ((jitW 0x66); jitxRex[1;xs;rd]),(`int$0x0f7e),jitxModRR[xs;rd]}

// ---- the two-pass branch resolution. `off` is the byte offset of a 4-byte displacement field
// inside an already-emitted instruction; an x86 relative displacement is measured from the end of
// the instruction it belongs to, which is exactly off+4.
jitxPatch32: {[asm;off;target]
  b: jitxLE32 (target-(off+4))
  asm[off]: b[0]
  asm[off+1]: b[1]
  asm[off+2]: b[2]
  asm[off+3]: b[3]
  asm}
// A branch whose target is already known (a back edge, a stub's exit): same fixed-width form,
// resolved on the spot instead of through a fixup list.
jitxJmpTo: {[asm;target] jitxPatch32[asm,jitxJmp[]; (count asm)+1; target]}

// ---- locals in registers (docs/compiler.md "Stage 2"). Identical scheme to arm64.nt's, with a narrower
// register window: `jitLocalSlots` (arm64.nt) hands out one register per slot the bytecode touches
// as a local, in first-seen order, and everything past jitxLREGS stays in `buf` and is reached
// through rbx per access. The prologue loads the register-resident ones once and nothing ever
// writes them back — sound for the same reason it is there: `Compiled::run` (src/jit.rs) reads only
// the returned value and the ok word, and neither entry point looks at `buf` again, so a return or
// a deopt just abandons the registers.
jitxLocalReg: {[st;a]
  p: (st[`lslots])?a
  $[p<count st[`lregs]; (st[`lregs])[p]; null]}
jitxLocalToReg: {[st;asm;rd;a]
  r: jitxLocalReg[st;a]
  $[isnull r; asm,jitxLoad[rd;jitxLOC;a*8]; asm,jitxMovRR[rd;r]]}
jitxRegToLocal: {[st;asm;a;rs]
  r: jitxLocalReg[st;a]
  $[isnull r; asm,jitxStore[jitxLOC;a*8;rs]; asm,jitxMovRR[r;rs]]}

// Around a `call`: every live operand-stack register goes to the frame and comes back, because the
// trampoline may clobber all of them and because this ABI's argument registers *are* four of them
// (see the header comment). `n` here is the whole virtual depth on the way in — including the
// operands the call is about to consume, which is how the argument setup reads them without
// caring what register they were in. On the way back only the ones the call didn't consume are
// restored; the result goes straight into the register the shortened stack leaves free.
// The local registers need nothing either way: both are callee-saved.
jitxSaveLive: {[asm;n]
  i: 0
  while[i<n; asm: asm,jitxStore[jitxSP; jitxSPILLBASE+(i*8); jitxSTACK+i]; i: i+1]
  asm}
jitxRestoreLive: {[asm;n]
  i: 0
  while[i<n; asm: asm,jitxLoad[jitxSTACK+i; jitxSP; jitxSPILLBASE+(i*8)]; i: i+1]
  asm}

// The six callee-saved registers, saved and restored unconditionally (see jitxSAVED).
jitxEmitPrologue: {[asm]
  i: 0
  while[i<count jitxSAVED; asm: asm,jitxPush jitxSAVED[i]; i: i+1]
  asm,jitxSubSP jitxFRAME}
jitxEmitEpilogue: {[asm]
  asm: asm,jitxAddSP jitxFRAME
  i: count jitxSAVED
  while[i>0; i: i-1; asm: asm,jitxPop jitxSAVED[i]]
  asm,jitxRet[]}

// The return value goes in rax and the ok word is set through r13; locals are not stored back
// (see the locals-in-registers comment above).
jitxEmitReturn: {[asm;valreg]
  asm: asm,jitxMovRR[jitxSCRATCH;valreg]
  asm: asm,jitxMovRI[jitxOKTMP;1]
  asm: asm,jitxStore[jitxOK;0;jitxOKTMP]
  jitxEmitEpilogue asm}
jitxEmitDeopt: {[asm]
  asm: asm,jitxMovRI[jitxOKTMP;0]
  asm: asm,jitxStore[jitxOK;0;jitxOKTMP]
  jitxEmitEpilogue asm}

// State carried through the walk. Same fields as arm64.nt's jitNewState, with two differences the
// encoding forces: a fixup is (displacement field offset; bytecode target) rather than (word
// index; target; isCbz), and the deopt fixups are bare offsets. Both because the *instruction* is
// already fully emitted by the time it goes on the list — on AArch64 the branch word could be
// re-encoded from scratch at patch time, here only its rel32 field is written, so the opcode (and
// the `test` that sets the flags a conditional branch reads) is chosen at emission time. The two
// AArch64 deopt kinds collapse into one for the same reason: `cmp dst, rbp` and `test rcx, rcx`
// both leave "deopt" as ZF=1, so both branches are a `jz` and neither needs to be told apart here.
jitxNewState: {[asm;lslots;lregs]
  `asm`depth`fixups`deoptFixups`depthAt`pending`lslots`lregs!(asm;0;();();()!();null;lslots;lregs)}

// ---- per-op handlers, one per arm64.nt handler, same contract: take the state dict, return the
// updated one, or null to reject the whole function.

// The one check arm64.nt doesn't make, and why: the register above the operand-stack file is the
// vm pointer here, not a scratch register. A push at the top of the file writes one register past
// it, which on AArch64 lands in x15 (scratch, reloaded before every use, so writing it early is
// harmless) and here would land on r12. The walk's own depth check catches the overflow at the
// *next* op and rejects the whole function, so nothing wrong is ever executed either way — but a
// backend shouldn't emit a store to a live pointer at all, so both stack-growing ops refuse the
// seventh operand themselves. It cannot change which functions compile: a function whose last op
// is a push has a complete expression on the stack, so it is at depth 1, not at the top of the
// file, and any push not at the end is followed by an op that makes the same check.
jitxOpPush: {[st;ip;opcodes;consts;a]
  if[not (st[`depth])<jitMAXDEPTH; :null]
  c: consts[a]
  $[isnull c
    ; $[not (opcodes[ip+1])=11; null   // Null is only ever immediately Pop'd — see jitOpPush
        ; st[`asm]: st[`asm],(); st[`depth]: (st[`depth])+1; st]
    ; not (`int)=type c
    ; null
    ; c=0N
    ; null
    ; st[`asm]: (st[`asm]),jitxMovRI[jitxSTACK+(st[`depth]); c]; st[`depth]: (st[`depth])+1; st]}

jitxOpLoadL: {[st;a]
  if[not (st[`depth])<jitMAXDEPTH; :null]
  if[not a<4096; :null]
  st[`asm]: jitxLocalToReg[st; st[`asm]; jitxSTACK+(st[`depth]); a]
  st[`depth]: (st[`depth])+1
  st}

// Doesn't pop — same as the interpreter's own `Op::StoreL`, which only peeks the top.
jitxOpStoreL: {[st;a]
  if[(st[`depth])<1; :null]
  if[not a<4096; :null]
  st[`asm]: jitxRegToLocal[st; st[`asm]; a; jitxSTACK+((st[`depth])-1)]
  st}

jitxOpPop: {[st]
  if[(st[`depth])<1; :null]
  st[`depth]: (st[`depth])-1
  st}

jitxOpRet: {[st]
  if[(st[`depth])<1; :null]
  st[`asm]: jitxEmitReturn[st[`asm]; jitxSTACK+((st[`depth])-1)]
  st}

// `x` is the top of the stack and the dyad's *left* argument, `y` the one below it, and the result
// has to end up in `y`'s register — the position the shortened stack leaves on top. That is what
// makes subtraction the one case that needs a temporary: every other form either commutes
// (`add`/`imul`), overwrites its destination from a flag test (`cmov`, `setcc`) or is a compare.
// `+ - *` then check the result against the int-null sentinel in rbp and deopt on a match, exactly
// as jitOpDyad does with x20.
jitxOpDyad: {[st;consts;a]
  if[(st[`depth])<2; :null]
  p: consts[a]
  kind: jitDyadKind string p
  if[isnull kind; :null]
  x: jitxSTACK+((st[`depth])-1)
  y: jitxSTACK+((st[`depth])-2)
  dst: y
  asm: st[`asm]
  df: st[`deoptFixups]
  $[kind=0
    ; asm: asm,jitxAddRR[dst;x]
    ; kind=1
    ; asm: ((asm,jitxMovRR[jitxSCRATCH;x]),jitxSubRR[jitxSCRATCH;y]),jitxMovRR[dst;jitxSCRATCH]
    ; kind=2
    ; asm: asm,jitxImulRR[dst;x]
    ; kind=3
    ; asm: (asm,jitxCmpRR[x;y]),jitxCmovcc[jitxCcL;dst;x]
    ; kind=4
    ; asm: (asm,jitxCmpRR[x;y]),jitxCmovcc[jitxCcG;dst;x]
    ; kind=5
    ; asm: ((asm,jitxCmpRR[x;y]),jitxSetcc[jitxCcL;dst]),jitxMovzx[dst;dst]
    ; kind=6
    ; asm: ((asm,jitxCmpRR[x;y]),jitxSetcc[jitxCcG;dst]),jitxMovzx[dst;dst]
    ; asm: ((asm,jitxCmpRR[x;y]),jitxSetcc[jitxCcE;dst]),jitxMovzx[dst;dst]]
  if[kind<3
    ; asm: asm,jitxCmpRR[dst;jitxNI]
      asm: asm,jitxJcc jitxCcE
      df: df,(count asm)-4]
  st[`asm]: asm
  st[`deoptFixups]: df
  st[`depth]: (st[`depth])-1
  st}

jitxOpJmp: {[st;t]
  st[`depthAt;t]: st[`depth]
  st[`asm]: (st[`asm]),jitxJmp[]
  st[`fixups]: (st[`fixups]),enlist ((count st[`asm])-4;t)
  st}

// `Jmpf` jumps when the condition is false, i.e. when the register is zero: `test`+`jz`, the pair
// AArch64 spells CBZ.
jitxOpJmpf: {[st;t]
  if[(st[`depth])<1; :null]
  r: jitxSTACK+((st[`depth])-1)
  st[`depth]: (st[`depth])-1
  st[`depthAt;t]: st[`depth]
  st[`asm]: ((st[`asm]),jitxTestRR[r;r]),jitxJcc jitxCcE
  st[`fixups]: (st[`fixups]),enlist ((count st[`asm])-4;t)
  st}

// do[n;..]: n>0 -> decrement in place and fall through (depth unchanged); else pop and jump to the
// exit (depth-1 — the one thing the naive linear scan can't see, so it's recorded in depthAt).
// One branch rather than AArch64's B.GT-over-a-B: x86 has the `jle` this wants directly.
jitxOpLoop: {[st;t]
  if[(st[`depth])<1; :null]
  r: jitxSTACK+((st[`depth])-1)
  asm: st[`asm]
  asm: asm,jitxCmpRI8[r;0]
  asm: asm,jitxJcc jitxCcLE
  st[`depthAt;t]: (st[`depth])-1
  st[`fixups]: (st[`fixups]),enlist ((count asm)-4;t)
  st[`asm]: asm,jitxSubRI8[r;1]
  st}

// jit_call(vm; slot; argc; arg0; arg1; &ok) -> rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8, r9, result in rax, and the
// ok word read back through rcx right after. The arguments come out of the spill slots rather
// than out of the operand-stack registers (see jitxSaveLive).
jitxOpCall: {[st;consts;trampoline;slot;argc]
  if[(st[`depth])<argc; :null]
  d: st[`depth]
  live: d-argc
  asm: jitxSaveLive[st[`asm]; d]
  asm: asm,jitxMovRR[7;jitxVM]
  asm: asm,jitxMovRI[6;slot]
  asm: asm,jitxMovRI[2;argc]
  asm: asm,jitxLoad[1; jitxSP; jitxSPILLBASE+((d-1)*8)]
  if[argc=2; asm: asm,jitxLoad[8; jitxSP; jitxSPILLBASE+((d-2)*8)]]
  asm: asm,jitxLeaSP[9;jitxCALLOK]
  asm: asm,jitxMovRI[jitxSCRATCH;trampoline]
  asm: asm,jitxCallR jitxSCRATCH
  asm: asm,jitxLoad[jitxOKTMP;jitxSP;jitxCALLOK]
  asm: asm,jitxTestRR[jitxOKTMP;jitxOKTMP]
  asm: asm,jitxJcc jitxCcE
  st[`deoptFixups]: (st[`deoptFixups]),(count asm)-4
  asm: asm,jitxMovRR[jitxSTACK+live; jitxSCRATCH]
  st[`asm]: jitxRestoreLive[asm; live]
  st[`depth]: live+1
  st[`pending]: null
  st}

// x[i]: jit_vec_get(vp; idx; &ok) -> rdi, rsi, rdx. The vector pointer comes from the slot's own
// register (callee-saved, so the call cannot touch it) or from `buf` for an overflow slot. Same
// deopt-on-zero-ok convention as a call: out of range, or defensively the wrong type.
jitxOpVecGet: {[st;vecgetAddr;slot]
  if[(st[`depth])<1; :null]
  d: st[`depth]
  live: d-1
  asm: jitxSaveLive[st[`asm]; d]
  asm: jitxLocalToReg[st;asm;7;slot]
  asm: asm,jitxLoad[6; jitxSP; jitxSPILLBASE+((d-1)*8)]
  asm: asm,jitxLeaSP[2;jitxCALLOK]
  asm: asm,jitxMovRI[jitxSCRATCH;vecgetAddr]
  asm: asm,jitxCallR jitxSCRATCH
  asm: asm,jitxLoad[jitxOKTMP;jitxSP;jitxCALLOK]
  asm: asm,jitxTestRR[jitxOKTMP;jitxOKTMP]
  asm: asm,jitxJcc jitxCcE
  st[`deoptFixups]: (st[`deoptFixups]),(count asm)-4
  asm: asm,jitxMovRR[jitxSTACK+live; jitxSCRATCH]
  st[`asm]: jitxRestoreLive[asm; live]
  st[`depth]: live+1
  st[`pending]: null
  st}

// x[i]:v: jit_vec_set(vp; idx; val; &ok) -> rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx. No return value — the trampoline
// mutates the vector in place, copy-on-write, so the `StoreL` this always precedes is a no-op.
// Net depth is -1, not -2, for the reason jitOpVecSet spells out: the interpreter's own `Op::StoreL`
// peeks rather than pops, so the slot has to exist for `Pop`'s bookkeeping even though nothing
// ever reads it.
jitxOpVecSet: {[st;vecsetAddr;slot]
  if[(st[`depth])<2; :null]
  d: st[`depth]
  live: d-2
  asm: jitxSaveLive[st[`asm]; d]
  asm: jitxLocalToReg[st;asm;7;slot]
  asm: asm,jitxLoad[6; jitxSP; jitxSPILLBASE+((d-1)*8)]
  asm: asm,jitxLoad[2; jitxSP; jitxSPILLBASE+((d-2)*8)]
  asm: asm,jitxLeaSP[1;jitxCALLOK]
  asm: asm,jitxMovRI[jitxSCRATCH;vecsetAddr]
  asm: asm,jitxCallR jitxSCRATCH
  asm: asm,jitxLoad[jitxOKTMP;jitxSP;jitxCALLOK]
  asm: asm,jitxTestRR[jitxOKTMP;jitxOKTMP]
  asm: asm,jitxJcc jitxCcE
  st[`deoptFixups]: (st[`deoptFixups]),(count asm)-4
  st[`asm]: jitxRestoreLive[asm; live]
  st[`depth]: live+1
  st[`pending]: (`vecwritedone;slot)
  st}

jitCompileX86: {[input;trampolines]
  opcodes: input[0]; opargs: input[1]; consts: input[2]; arity: input[3]; nlocals: input[4]
  n: count opcodes
  callAddr: trampolines[0]; vecgetAddr: trampolines[1]; vecsetAddr: trampolines[2]
  // This backend has no `r: n#0` allocation support (see jitVecAllocs in arm64.nt), so it passes
  // empty lists and classifies exactly what it always did.
  vecSlots: jitClassifySlots[opcodes;opargs;n;arity;nlocals;`int$();`int$()]
  lslots: jitLocalSlots[opcodes;opargs;n]
  // Same bound arm64.nt checks: a slot is reached in `buf` at a fixed displacement at least once
  // (the prologue load, or every access for an overflow slot). disp32 could reach far further
  // than this, but the two backends rejecting different functions would be worse than the room.
  p: 0
  while[p<count lslots; if[not (lslots[p])<4096; :null]; p: p+1]
  lregs: ((count lslots) & count jitxLREGS)#jitxLREGS

  asm: jitxEmitPrologue[()]
  asm: asm,jitxMovRR[jitxLOC;7]     // rbx <- rdi, the locals buffer
  asm: asm,jitxMovRR[jitxOK;6]      // r13 <- rsi, the ok word
  asm: asm,jitxMovRR[jitxVM;2]      // r12 <- rdx, the Vm
  asm: asm,jitxMovRI[jitxNI;0N]
  // buf -> the local registers, once. Every one of them, not just the params/captures: a scratch
  // local's slot is the 0 `buf` was zeroed with, exactly what a load through rbx would have read
  // before its first StoreL.
  p: 0
  while[p<count lregs; asm: asm,jitxLoad[lregs[p]; jitxLOC; 8*lslots[p]]; p: p+1]

  st: jitxNewState[asm;lslots;lregs]
  ipByte: (n+1)#0   // n+1: a jump target can be the position right after the last op
  ip: 0
  while[ip<n
    if[not isnull (st[`depthAt])[ip]; st[`depth]: (st[`depthAt])[ip]]
    ipByte[ip]: count st[`asm]
    if[(st[`depth])<0; :null]
    if[(st[`depth])>jitMAXDEPTH; :null]
    op: opcodes[ip]
    a: opargs[ip]
    pending: st[`pending]
    pendKind: $[isnull pending; null; pending[0]]
    isVecSlot: a in vecSlots
    isCallHead: (op=7) & ((a=1)|(a=2))
    isAmendHead: (op=15) & (a=1)
    isStoreHead: $[isnull pending; 0b; (pendKind=`vecwritedone) & (op=4) & (a=(pending[1]))]
    okNext: $[isnull pending; 1b
             ; pendKind=`call; isCallHead
             ; pendKind=`vecread; isCallHead
             ; pendKind=`vecwrite; isAmendHead
             ; pendKind=`vecwritedone; isStoreHead
             ; 0b]
    if[not okNext; :null]

    $[op=0; st: jitxOpPush[st;ip;opcodes;consts;a]
    ; (op=3) & isVecSlot; st[`pending]: (`vecread;a)
    ; op=3; st: jitxOpLoadL[st;a]
    ; isStoreHead; st[`pending]: null
    ; op=4; st: jitxOpStoreL[st;a]
    ; op=11; st: jitxOpPop st
    ; op=16; st: jitxOpRet st
    ; op=6; st: jitxOpDyad[st;consts;a]
    ; op=9; st: jitxOpJmp[st;a]
    ; op=10; st: jitxOpJmpf[st;a]
    ; op=18; st: jitxOpLoop[st;a]
    ; (op=13) & isVecSlot; st[`pending]: (`vecwrite;a)
    ; op=1
    ; $[not (`int)=type consts[a]; st: null
        ; st[`pending]: (`call;consts[a])]
    ; (pendKind=`vecwrite) & isAmendHead; st: jitxOpVecSet[st;vecsetAddr;pending[1]]
    ; (pendKind=`call) & isCallHead; st: jitxOpCall[st;consts;callAddr;pending[1];a]
    ; (pendKind=`vecread) & isCallHead; st: jitxOpVecGet[st;vecgetAddr;pending[1]]
    ; st: null]

    if[isnull st; :null]
    ip: ip+1]

  if[not isnull st[`pending]; :null]
  ipByte[n]: count st[`asm]

  lastOp: $[n>0; opcodes[n-1]; -1]
  if[not lastOp=16
    ; if[(st[`depth])<1; :null]
      st[`asm]: jitxEmitReturn[st[`asm]; jitxSTACK+((st[`depth])-1)]]

  deoptAt: count st[`asm]
  asm: jitxEmitDeopt[st[`asm]]

  // Pass two: every branch above was emitted in full with a zero rel32, so this only writes
  // displacements — four bytes at a recorded offset, nothing moves (see the header comment).
  fixups: st[`fixups]
  i: 0
  while[i<count fixups
    e: fixups[i]
    asm: jitxPatch32[asm; e[0]; ipByte[e[1]]]
    i: i+1]
  deoptFixups: st[`deoptFixups]
  i: 0
  while[i<count deoptFixups
    asm: jitxPatch32[asm; deoptFixups[i]; deoptAt]
    i: i+1]

  (`byte$asm; vecSlots)}


// ==== tracing JIT (src/trace.rs): compile one recorded loop iteration ====
//
// jitCompileTraceX86[input] -> (Bytes; Ints; Ints; Int; List) or null, exactly jitCompileTrace's
// contract — same input encoding, same result shape, same buffer layout decided here and read back
// by src/jit.rs. Read jitCompileTrace's section comment in arm64.nt for what a trace *is* and why
// the exits, the rewind stub and the loop-carried stack look the way they do; only the register
// assignment, the encoders and two consequences of the ISA differ, and those are what this comment
// covers.
//
// ---- registers. A compiled trace makes no calls at all (a call in the loop is *inlined* by the
// recorder), so on AArch64 it needs no callee-saved register and no prologue. There are not enough
// caller-saved registers on this ABI for that: nine, against a buffer pointer, an out pointer, a
// sentinel, a scratch, six operand-stack slots and one register per local. So this tier *does*
// have a prologue — `push` of the same six callee-saved registers the whole-function tier saves,
// popped at the one tail every exit funnels through. That is paid once per entry into the trace,
// not once per iteration, and it buys the loop body the registers it needs:
//
//   rdi  the buffer (touched locals in and out, then the operand-stack area) — src/jit.rs's
//        `CompiledTrace`; first argument, held for the trace's whole lifetime
//   rsi  the out pointer (which exit index got there); second argument, likewise
//   rbx  the int-null sentinel, compared after every int add/sub/mul exactly as x17 is
//   rax  scratch: the exit index in each stub, and the staging register a float constant's raw
//        bits go through on their way into an xmm (x86 has no float-immediate form)
//   r8..r13    the int operand stack (jitMAXDEPTH deep), consecutive like the other tier's
//   xmm8..xmm13  the float operand stack, mirroring it position for position
//   rcx,rdx,rbp,r14,r15  one per int local, in first-seen order (jitxTrLOCALS)
//   xmm0..xmm7           one per float local (jitxTrFLOCALS)
//   xmm15                the temporary float subtraction needs (see jitxTrDyadFArith)
//
// Five int locals against AArch64's eight: a loop body wider than that just doesn't get traced,
// the same fail-closed way one nested deeper than jitMAXDEPTH doesn't.
//
// ---- `&`/`|` on floats are rejected here, not compiled. On AArch64 they are FMINNM/FMAXNM,
// which is exactly IEEE minNum/maxNum — the behaviour of Rust's `f64::min`/`max`
// (`min2`/`max2`, src/prims.rs), which propagate the non-NaN side when only one operand is a NaN.
// SSE2's `minsd`/`maxsd` are *not* that: they return the second operand whenever either is a NaN,
// so they disagree with the interpreter on `0n & 1.0`. Emulating minNum takes a compare, two
// branches and a NaN case, for an operation no measured loop does; refusing the trace instead
// leaves that loop interpreted and correct, which is the same fail-closed default as everything
// else here. Float `+ - *` and the comparisons compile.
//
// ---- the float comparisons go through `ucomisd` and the *unsigned* condition codes, and the
// reason is NaN. `ucomisd a, b` sets CF/ZF as an unsigned compare would and additionally sets PF
// when either operand is a NaN — in which case ZF, CF and PF are all 1. So `sete` after it would
// call two NaNs equal, and `setb` would call a NaN less than everything. `seta` (CF=0 and ZF=0) is
// true only for an *ordered* strictly-greater, which is why `x<y` compiles to `ucomisd y, x` plus
// `seta` — the operands swapped rather than the condition inverted. Equality needs the parity flag
// itself: `sete` and `setnp` and'ed together, the one place this backend's `and` is what the
// language's `=` actually needs (the integer `&`/`|` verbs are min/max — `cmp`+`cmovcc` — not
// bitwise).
//
// ---- and the stubs are emitted, not predicted. arm64.nt computes where each exit stub will land
// before emitting any of them, because there its every instruction is one word wide. Here a stub's
// length depends on what it stores, so instead each stub is emitted and its actual start offset
// recorded, and the forward branches *inside* the stubs (to the shared writeback, or past it to
// the tail) join the same fixup list as everything else. Emit, record, patch — see the top of this
// file on why that is exact.

jitxTrBUF: 7        // rdi
jitxTrOUT: 6        // rsi
jitxTrNIREG: 3      // rbx
jitxTrSTACK: 8      // r8..r13
jitxTrFSTACK: 8     // xmm8..xmm13
jitxTrLOCALS: 1 2 5 14 15
jitxTrFLOCALS: 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7
jitxTrFSCRATCH: 15  // xmm15

// One register per local, by position, int and float files handed out independently in first-seen
// order — jitTrLocalRegs (arm64.nt) with this backend's two files. Null if either runs out.
jitxTrLocalRegs: {[stys]
  regs: ()
  ni: 0; nf: 0
  p: 0
  while[p<count stys
    isF: (stys[p])=1
    if[isF
      ; if[not nf<count jitxTrFLOCALS; :null]
        regs: regs,jitxTrFLOCALS[nf]
        nf: nf+1]
    if[not isF
      ; if[not ni<count jitxTrLOCALS; :null]
        regs: regs,jitxTrLOCALS[ni]
        ni: ni+1]
    p: p+1]
  regs}

// The local registers -> the buffer, for the positions in `which`: the exit path (what makes the
// locals visible to the interpreter again) and, only when the trace has a rewind in it, the top of
// every iteration.
jitxTrStoreLocals: {[asm;which;stys;lregs]
  i: 0
  while[i<count which
    p: which[i]
    $[(stys[p])=1
    ; asm: asm,jitxMovsdStore[jitxTrBUF; p*8; lregs[p]]
    ; asm: asm,jitxStore[jitxTrBUF; p*8; lregs[p]]]
    i: i+1]
  asm}
// The buffer -> the local registers, once on entry. Every local of the loop's own frame; an
// inlined callee's local has nothing to load (its first write is inside the loop).
jitxTrLoadLocals: {[asm;slots;stys;lregs;realUpto]
  p: 0
  while[p<count stys
    if[(slots[p])<realUpto
      ; $[(stys[p])=1
        ; asm: asm,jitxMovsdLoad[lregs[p]; jitxTrBUF; p*8]
        ; asm: asm,jitxLoad[lregs[p]; jitxTrBUF; p*8]]]
    p: p+1]
  asm}

// The operand stack <-> the buffer's stack area, one word per live value, bottom first: value j at
// word `stackBase+j`, in the register its file and its position among that file's values below it
// put it in (jitTrPos's numbering). A null placeholder is skipped — nothing was ever written to
// its register, and its tag alone tells the interpreter what to push.
jitxTrStoreStack: {[asm;tags;stackBase]
  j: 0
  while[j<count tags
    t: tags[j]
    reg: jitTrPos[j#tags;t]
    $[t=`null; asm
    ; t=`float; asm: asm,jitxMovsdStore[jitxTrBUF; (stackBase+j)*8; jitxTrFSTACK+reg]
    ; asm: asm,jitxStore[jitxTrBUF; (stackBase+j)*8; jitxTrSTACK+reg]]
    j: j+1]
  asm}
jitxTrLoadStack: {[asm;tags;stackBase]
  j: 0
  while[j<count tags
    t: tags[j]
    reg: jitTrPos[j#tags;t]
    $[t=`null; asm
    ; t=`float; asm: asm,jitxMovsdLoad[jitxTrFSTACK+reg; jitxTrBUF; (stackBase+j)*8]
    ; asm: asm,jitxLoad[jitxTrSTACK+reg; jitxTrBUF; (stackBase+j)*8]]
    j: j+1]
  asm}

// The branch a guard leaves the loop on — the condition under which the recorded direction is
// *not* the one being taken this time, in jitTrBranch's own numbering: 0 = the `Jmpf` condition
// went false, 1 = it went true, 2 = an int result hit the null sentinel (after a cmp), 3/4 = a `do`
// counter that is not / is positive (after a cmp against 0). Kinds 0 and 1 need the flags a `test`
// sets, so unlike AArch64's single CBZ/CBNZ word this emits two instructions — which is why the
// branch kind is decided here, at emission time, and only the displacement is left for the patch.
jitxTrBranchEmit: {[asm;kind;reg]
  $[kind=0; (asm,jitxTestRR[reg;reg]),jitxJcc jitxCcE
  ; kind=1; (asm,jitxTestRR[reg;reg]),jitxJcc jitxCcNE
  ; kind=2; asm,jitxJcc jitxCcE
  ; kind=3; asm,jitxJcc jitxCcLE
  ; asm,jitxJcc jitxCcG]}

// The guard word (now: the guard's branch) leaving through exit `exitIdx`, or through the one
// rewind stub. jitTrExit (arm64.nt) records the exit itself — pure bookkeeping, shared.
jitxTrGuardWord: {[st;exitIdx;branchKind;reg]
  st[`asm]: jitxTrBranchEmit[st[`asm];branchKind;reg]
  st[`fixups]: (st[`fixups]),enlist ((count st[`asm])-4; exitIdx)
  st}
jitxTrRewindWord: {[st;branchKind;reg]
  st[`asm]: jitxTrBranchEmit[st[`asm];branchKind;reg]
  st[`rewFixups]: (st[`rewFixups]),(count st[`asm])-4
  st}

jitxTrPush: {[st;consts;a;ty]
  tstack: st[`tstack]
  reg: jitTrPos[tstack;ty]
  if[not reg<jitMAXDEPTH; :null]
  c: consts[a]
  isFloat: ty=`float
  if[not $[isFloat; (`float)=type c; ty=`int; (`int)=type c; 0b]; :null]
  if[(not isFloat) & (c=0N); :null]
  asm: st[`asm]
  // No float-immediate form: the bits go through the scratch GPR, the way AArch64 goes through
  // x15 and FMOV.
  $[isFloat
  ; asm: (asm,jitxMovRI[jitxSCRATCH; fbits c]),jitxMovqXR[jitxTrFSTACK+reg; jitxSCRATCH]
  ; asm: asm,jitxMovRI[jitxTrSTACK+reg; c]]
  st[`asm]: asm
  st[`tstack]: tstack,ty
  st}

jitxTrPushNull: {[st] st[`tstack]: (st[`tstack]),`null; st}

jitxTrLoadL: {[st;slots;lregs;a;ty]
  tstack: st[`tstack]
  reg: jitTrPos[tstack;ty]
  if[not reg<jitMAXDEPTH; :null]
  pos: slots?a
  if[pos=count slots; :null]
  asm: st[`asm]
  $[ty=`float
  ; asm: asm,jitxMovsdRR[jitxTrFSTACK+reg; lregs[pos]]
  ; asm: asm,jitxMovRR[jitxTrSTACK+reg; lregs[pos]]]
  st[`asm]: asm
  st[`tstack]: tstack,ty
  st}

// Doesn't pop — the interpreter's own `Op::StoreL` only peeks the top.
jitxTrStoreL: {[st;slots;lregs;a;ty]
  tstack: st[`tstack]
  n: count tstack
  if[n<1; :null]
  if[not (tstack[n-1])=ty; :null]
  reg: (jitTrPos[tstack;ty])-1
  pos: slots?a
  if[pos=count slots; :null]
  asm: st[`asm]
  $[ty=`float
  ; asm: asm,jitxMovsdRR[lregs[pos]; jitxTrFSTACK+reg]
  ; asm: asm,jitxMovRR[lregs[pos]; jitxTrSTACK+reg]]
  st[`asm]: asm
  st}

jitxTrPop: {[st]
  tstack: st[`tstack]
  n: count tstack
  if[n<1; :null]
  st[`tstack]: (n-1)#tstack
  st}

// x is the top of the stack and y the one below it, and the top is a dyad's *left* argument, so
// `x-y` — not `y-x` — is what these emit. See the section comment on the condition codes.
jitxTrDyadFCmp: {[asm;x;y;dst;kind]
  $[kind=5
  ; ((asm,jitxUcomisd[y;x]),jitxSetcc[jitxCcA;dst]),jitxMovzx[dst;dst]
  ; kind=6
  ; ((asm,jitxUcomisd[x;y]),jitxSetcc[jitxCcA;dst]),jitxMovzx[dst;dst]
  ; asm: (asm,jitxUcomisd[x;y]),jitxSetcc[jitxCcE;dst]
    asm: (asm,jitxSetcc[jitxCcNP;jitxSCRATCH]),jitxMovzx[dst;dst]
    (asm,jitxMovzx[jitxSCRATCH;jitxSCRATCH]),jitxAndRR[dst;jitxSCRATCH]]}
jitxTrDyadICmp: {[asm;x;y;dst;kind]
  cc: $[kind=5;jitxCcL; kind=6;jitxCcG; jitxCcE]
  ((asm,jitxCmpRR[x;y]),jitxSetcc[cc;dst]),jitxMovzx[dst;dst]}
// `&`/`|` never reach here (jitxTrDyad rejects them for floats — see the section comment), so this
// is `+ - *` only, and only `-` needs a temporary: addsd/mulsd commute onto dst, subsd does not.
jitxTrDyadFArith: {[asm;x;y;dst;kind]
  $[kind=0; asm,jitxAddsd[dst;x]
  ; kind=1; ((asm,jitxMovsdRR[jitxTrFSCRATCH;x]),jitxSubsd[jitxTrFSCRATCH;y]),jitxMovsdRR[dst;jitxTrFSCRATCH]
  ; asm,jitxMulsd[dst;x]]}
jitxTrDyadIArith: {[asm;x;y;dst;kind]
  $[kind=0; asm,jitxAddRR[dst;x]
  ; kind=1; ((asm,jitxMovRR[jitxSCRATCH;x]),jitxSubRR[jitxSCRATCH;y]),jitxMovRR[dst;jitxSCRATCH]
  ; kind=2; asm,jitxImulRR[dst;x]
  ; kind=3; (asm,jitxCmpRR[x;y]),jitxCmovcc[jitxCcL;dst;x]
  ; (asm,jitxCmpRR[x;y]),jitxCmovcc[jitxCcG;dst;x]]}

// Type rules, the null-collision exit and the reject cases are jitTrDyad's, unchanged; see its
// comment. The one addition is the float `&`/`|` refusal (section comment).
jitxTrDyad: {[st;consts;a;ty;rip]
  tstack: st[`tstack]
  n: count tstack
  if[n<2; :null]
  opTy: tstack[n-1]; opTy2: tstack[n-2]
  if[not (opTy in `int`bool`float) & (opTy2 in `int`bool`float); :null]
  isFloat: opTy=`float
  if[not isFloat=(opTy2=`float); :null]
  p: consts[a]
  kind: jitDyadKind string p
  if[isnull kind; :null]
  if[isFloat & ((kind=3)|(kind=4)); :null]
  isCmp: kind>4
  bothBool: (opTy=`bool)&(opTy2=`bool)
  resultTy: $[isCmp; `bool; isFloat; `float; bothBool & ((kind=3)|(kind=4)); `bool; `int]
  if[not resultTy=ty; :null]
  base: $[isFloat; jitxTrFSTACK; jitxTrSTACK]
  x: base+((jitTrPos[tstack;opTy])-1)
  y: base+((jitTrPos[tstack;opTy])-2)
  newTstack: (n-2)#tstack
  dst: $[isCmp; jitxTrSTACK+(jitTrPos[newTstack;`int]); y]
  asm: $[isCmp & isFloat; jitxTrDyadFCmp[st[`asm];x;y;dst;kind]
       ; isCmp; jitxTrDyadICmp[st[`asm];x;y;dst;kind]
       ; isFloat; jitxTrDyadFArith[st[`asm];x;y;dst;kind]
       ; jitxTrDyadIArith[st[`asm];x;y;dst;kind]]
  st[`asm]: asm
  st[`tstack]: newTstack,resultTy
  // Two ordinary ints wrapping to exactly the null sentinel (`0W+1`) — the interpreter would
  // propagate a null from here and this backend would keep doing plain wrapping arithmetic. Exit
  // to the op after this one with the stack as it stands; inside an inlined callee that ip is in
  // another frame's bytecode, so it rewinds the iteration instead. Only `+ - *` can do it.
  if[(not isFloat) & (kind<3)
    ; st[`asm]: (st[`asm]),jitxCmpRR[dst;jitxTrNIREG]
      inFrame: 0<count st[`frames]
      if[inFrame; st: jitxTrRewindWord[st;2;0]]
      if[not inFrame
        ; st: jitTrExit[st;rip;st[`tstack]]
          st: jitxTrGuardWord[st; (count st[`exits])-1; 2; 0]]]
  st}

jitxTrGuard: {[st;taken;bailIp]
  tstack: st[`tstack]
  n: count tstack
  if[n<1; :null]
  if[not (tstack[n-1]) in `int`bool; :null]
  reg: jitxTrSTACK+((jitTrPos[tstack;`int])-1)
  rest: (n-1)#tstack
  st: jitTrExit[st;bailIp;rest]
  st: jitxTrGuardWord[st; (count st[`exits])-1; $[taken;1;0]; reg]
  st[`tstack]: rest
  st}

jitxTrGuardRewind: {[st;taken]
  tstack: st[`tstack]
  n: count tstack
  if[n<1; :null]
  if[not (tstack[n-1]) in `int`bool; :null]
  reg: jitxTrSTACK+((jitTrPos[tstack;`int])-1)
  st: jitxTrRewindWord[st; $[taken;1;0]; reg]
  st[`tstack]: (n-1)#tstack
  st}

jitxTrLoop: {[st;exited;bailIp]
  tstack: st[`tstack]
  n: count tstack
  if[n<1; :null]
  if[not (tstack[n-1])=`int; :null]
  reg: jitxTrSTACK+((jitTrPos[tstack;`int])-1)
  rest: (n-1)#tstack
  st[`asm]: (st[`asm]),jitxCmpRI8[reg;0]
  st: jitTrExit[st;bailIp;$[exited;tstack;rest]]
  st: jitxTrGuardWord[st; (count st[`exits])-1; $[exited;4;3]; reg]
  if[exited; st[`tstack]: rest]
  if[not exited; st[`asm]: (st[`asm]),jitxSubRI8[reg;1]]
  st}

jitxTrFramePush: {[st;nargs]
  n: count st[`tstack]
  if[n<nargs; :null]
  st[`frames]: (st[`frames]),(n-nargs)
  st}

jitxTrFrameEnd: {[st]
  frames: st[`frames]
  nf: count frames
  if[nf<1; :null]
  base: frames[nf-1]
  tstack: st[`tstack]
  n: count tstack
  if[n<(base+1); :null]
  ty: tstack[n-1]
  kept: base#tstack
  file: $[ty=`float; jitxTrFSTACK; jitxTrSTACK]
  src: file+((jitTrPos[tstack;ty])-1)
  dst: file+jitTrPos[kept;ty]
  asm: st[`asm]
  if[(not src=dst) & (not ty=`null)
    ; $[ty=`float; asm: asm,jitxMovsdRR[dst;src]; asm: asm,jitxMovRR[dst;src]]]
  st[`asm]: asm
  st[`tstack]: kept,ty
  st[`frames]: (nf-1)#frames
  st}

jitCompileTraceX86: {[input]
  kinds: input[0]; args: input[1]; tys: input[2]; ips: input[3]; consts: input[4]
  header: input[5]; realUpto: input[6]
  entry: $[0=count input[7]; (); jitTrTag each input[7]]
  n: count kinds
  touched: jitTraceTouched[kinds;args;tys;realUpto]
  if[isnull touched; :null]
  slots: touched[0]; stys: touched[1]; stored: touched[2]
  nslots: count slots
  if[not nslots<jitTrMAXTOUCHED; :null]
  lregs: jitxTrLocalRegs stys
  if[isnull lregs; :null]   // out of local registers
  stackBase: nslots

  // The body is assembled on its own first (offsets relative to its own start, fixed up by
  // `bodyAt` below): whether the top of the iteration has to store anything is known only once
  // the body has been walked, and everything it branches to is placed after it anyway.
  st: `asm`tstack`fixups`exits`rewFixups`frames!((); entry; (); (); (); ())
  i: 0
  while[i<n
    kind: kinds[i]; a: args[i]
    ty: jitTrTag tys[i]
    $[kind=0; st: jitxTrPush[st;consts;a;ty]
    ; kind=1; st: jitxTrPushNull st
    ; kind=2; st: jitxTrLoadL[st;slots;lregs;a;ty]
    ; kind=3; st: jitxTrStoreL[st;slots;lregs;a;ty]
    ; kind=4; st: jitxTrDyad[st;consts;a;ty;ips[i]]
    ; kind=5; st: jitxTrPop st
    ; kind=6; st: jitxTrGuard[st;1b;a]
    ; kind=7; st: jitxTrGuard[st;0b;a]
    ; kind=8; st: jitxTrGuardRewind[st;1b]
    ; kind=9; st: jitxTrGuardRewind[st;0b]
    ; kind=10; st: jitxTrFramePush[st;a]
    ; kind=11; st: jitxTrFrameEnd st
    ; kind=12; st: jitxTrLoop[st;0b;a]
    ; kind=13; st: jitxTrLoop[st;1b;a]
    ; st: null]
    if[isnull st; :null]
    i: i+1]

  if[not jitTrSameTags[st[`tstack];entry]; :null]
  if[not 0=count st[`frames]; :null]
  exits: st[`exits]
  hasRewind: 0<count st[`rewFixups]
  if[hasRewind; exits: exits,enlist (header; entry)]
  numExits: count exits
  if[numExits<1; :null]

  // Entry: save the callee-saved registers this tier uses (see the section comment — unlike
  // AArch64's version, it needs some), then the sentinel, the locals and the entry stack.
  asm: ()
  i: 0
  while[i<count jitxSAVED; asm: asm,jitxPush jitxSAVED[i]; i: i+1]
  asm: asm,jitxMovRI[jitxTrNIREG;0N]
  asm: jitxTrLoadLocals[asm;slots;stys;lregs;realUpto]
  asm: jitxTrLoadStack[asm;entry;stackBase]
  loopStart: count asm
  // Only a rewind needs the iteration's starting point kept anywhere; without one the loop body
  // has no memory operation in it at all.
  if[hasRewind
    ; asm: jitxTrStoreLocals[asm;stored;stys;lregs]
      asm: jitxTrStoreStack[asm;entry;stackBase]]
  bodyAt: count asm
  asm: asm,st[`asm]
  asm: jitxJmpTo[asm;loopStart]

  // One stub per exit — its index into rax, its live stack into the buffer's stack area, then the
  // shared writeback — emitted here and *measured*, not predicted (section comment). The rewind
  // stub skips the writeback: the buffer already holds what this iteration started with.
  starts: ()
  stubFix: ()
  ei: 0
  while[ei<numExits
    starts: starts,count asm
    isRew: hasRewind & (ei=(numExits-1))
    asm: asm,jitxMovRI[jitxSCRATCH;ei]
    if[not isRew; asm: jitxTrStoreStack[asm;(exits[ei])[1];stackBase]]
    asm: asm,jitxJmp[]
    stubFix: stubFix,enlist ((count asm)-4; isRew)
    ei: ei+1]
  writebackAt: count asm
  asm: jitxTrStoreLocals[asm;stored;stys;lregs]
  tailAt: count asm
  asm: asm,jitxStore[jitxTrOUT; 0; jitxSCRATCH]   // *out = the index of whichever exit got here
  i: count jitxSAVED
  while[i>0; i: i-1; asm: asm,jitxPop jitxSAVED[i]]
  asm: asm,jitxRet[]

  // Pass two, all three fixup lists: the stubs' forward branches, the guards' branches to their
  // stubs, and the rewinding guards' branches to the one rewind stub.
  i: 0
  while[i<count stubFix
    e: stubFix[i]
    asm: jitxPatch32[asm; e[0]; $[e[1]; tailAt; writebackAt]]
    i: i+1]
  fixups: st[`fixups]
  fi: 0
  while[fi<count fixups
    e: fixups[fi]
    asm: jitxPatch32[asm; bodyAt+e[0]; starts[e[1]]]
    fi: fi+1]
  rfix: st[`rewFixups]
  rewAt: starts[numExits-1]
  fi: 0
  while[fi<count rfix
    asm: jitxPatch32[asm; bodyAt+rfix[fi]; rewAt]
    fi: fi+1]

  (`byte$asm; `int$slots; `int$stys; stackBase; {(x[0]; jitTrTagCodes x[1])} each exits)}
